>> title: Chinese Yam, Goji and Black Chicken Soup
>> category: Soups
>> tags: black chicken, chinese yam, goji, red dates, cantonese, old-fire soup, winter, stovetop
>> counters: The Soup Pot
>> aka: 淮山杞子紅棗烏雞湯, waai saan gei zi hung zou wu gai tong, wai san gei ji hung jou wu gai tong, Chinese yam goji and black chicken soup, silkie chicken soup, 老火湯, lo fo tong
>> servings: 6
>> time: 3 hr 15 min
>> slack: narrow — the goji have about ten minutes in them; boiled the full three hours they burst and go sour, and they take the pot's sweetness with them
>> step.1: The winter tonic. 補氣 is the word the tradition uses for what this pot is for, and it is why it is made — the pot set on for someone recovering, or in the month after a birth.
>> step.2: blanch from cold, then rinse the bird
>> step.3: rinse the yam and the dates
>> step.4: simmer 2 1/2 hr, barely a quiver
>> step.5: stir the goji in, last 10 min
>> step.6: season at the end, never at the start
>> step.7: The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.

The winter tonic. 補氣 is the word the tradition uses for what this pot is for, and it is why it is made — the pot set on for someone recovering, or in the month after a birth.

Blanch @black chicken{2 1/2%lb}(1.1 kg; wu gai, the black-skinned bird, cut into pieces) from cold in @water{2%qt}(1.9 L) in a #soup pot{}, ~blanch{10%min} up to a rolling boil, then pour the whole lot away, rinse the pieces under the tap and scrub the pot out.

Rinse @dried Chinese yam{1%oz}(30 g; waai saan, sliced dried yam — 健脾, the spleen-and-stomach word, and the everyday tonic) and @red dates{6}(hung zou; pitted, because the stone is held to make a pot 燥) under cold water.

Simmer @&(~2)blanched chicken{} with @&(~1)yam and dates{}, @ginger{4%slices} and @cold water{3%qt}(2.8 L; the pot is filled once and not topped up) in the rinsed #soup pot{}, lid ajar, ~simmer{2 1/2%hr} at a bare quiver, unstirred.

Stir @goji berries{2%Tbs}(gei zi; they are already soft, which is why they go in last) into @&(~1)soup{} and ~simmer{10%min} more.

Season @&(~1)soup{} with @kosher salt{1%tsp}, at the end and never at the start.

The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.
